                <pb facs="0279" n="267" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg267"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00111A.R./938: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-01-17T16:00:00-03:00">January 17, 1940—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:24 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>26. For the Under Secretary from Fenwick. Neutrality Committee now organized
                    plans prompt decisions on concrete problems as per memorandum already in your
                    hands also special questions submitted by Uruguay and other governments. Attempt
                    will also be made to codify law of neutrality but this less urgent. Believe it
                    possible to make recommendations on immediate problems within 3 weeks. Will
                    forward preliminary recommendations on specific points within a few days.
                    [Fenwick.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
